# Preface

// This file contains a formalisation of Nova's type theory: Extensional Computational Type Theory.
// The presentation is by inference rules over a first-order grammar, and
// its OFFICIAL reading is COMPUTATIONAL, in the sense of NuPRL and
// Allen: the grammar is a prior universe of raw terms, and a type is a
// PARTIAL EQUIVALENCE RELATION on them. Every sort of the theory has
// two judgements, a UNARY one — Γ ⊦ a : A, "a is an element of A" —
// and a BINARY one — Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A, "a₀ and a₁ are related in A" —
// the binary an equivalence relation that PRESUPPOSES the unary of
// both its sides (see Judgements). The setoid model below is this
// reading made precise, the unary form as membership and the binary
// as the relation; canonicity-style consequences are stated as
// meta-properties where they are used.
//
// TYPES ARE TYPED BY THEIR LEVEL. The type judgement is Γ ⊦ A type ℓ,
// "A is a type of level at most ℓ", for ℓ a natural number or ω;
// Γ ⊦ A type is its ω instance, typehood with no bound (ω is an index
// of the judgement, not a level: there is no 𝕌ω). The universes form
// a CUMULATIVE TOWER 𝕌₀ : 𝕌₁ : 𝕌₂ : … (ℓ a natural-number literal),
// and the types of level ℓ are EXACTLY the elements of 𝕌ℓ — the
// UNIVERSE BRIDGE, univ-i/univ-e and their equation halves
// univ-eq-i/univ-eq-e in the type rules — with the universe of
// propositions Ω below the bottom: Ω : 𝕌₁, and every proposition a
// type of level 0 (see THE TOWER). Types and elements are ONE term
// sort. There is no top: every type has a FINITE level (a
// meta-theorem, Judgements), so the type slot of the equality
// proposition admits the universes, type-equality props (A ≡ B ∈ 𝕌ℓ)
// exist for any two types, and the level-free type equation
// Γ ⊦ A₀ ≐ A₁ type is reflection-complete like element equality (see
// code-eq and its SEMANTICS note in the Ω block).
//
// Semantics is assigned by the SETOID MODEL: a type denotes a
// structural CODE together with a proof-irrelevant equivalence relation
// on the code's decoding — type equality is equality of codes, element
// equality is the relation. The code layer interprets the structural
// fragment; the relation layer is where
// quotients, Ω-truth and the QIIT congruences live (see the SEMANTICS
// notes at their rules).
//
// NOTE: well-typed terms need not normalize in an inconsistent context.
// Equality reflection makes typability hypothesis-sensitive: e.g. under
// Γ ∋ h : (A ≡ (A → A) ∈ 𝕌ℓ), the equation A ≐ A → A holds (at 𝕌ℓ,
// and as a type equation by univ-eq-e), so
// self-application types and ω ω is well-formed at A, where ω ≔ λx. x x
// — yet ω ω has no normal form. Normalization is therefore a property of terms
// in consistent contexts only; any procedure that beta-normalizes a
// well-formed term (as the checker does when storing and comparing facts)
// may diverge under inconsistent hypotheses. This is a liveness caveat, not
// a soundness one: a non-terminating normalization never certifies a
// judgement.

# Type Theory

// We have:
//  * Empty type: 𝟘
//  * Unit type: 𝟙
//  * Natural numbers type: ℕ
//  * Dependent product type: (→)
//  * Dependent sum type: (×)
//  * Record type: Rec l̄ Δ — see "Record types"
//  * Non-dependent sum type: (⊎) — DEFINABLE as a QIIT (see
//    SUBSUMPTION in the QIIT notes); retained as a standalone former,
//    like ℕ and (/)
//  * Equality proposition: (≡), Ω-valued and DEFINITIONALLY reflected
//    (see the Ω block)
//  * Quotient type: (/), by an Ω-valued relation
//  * A cumulative tower of predicative universes: 𝕌₀ : 𝕌₁ : 𝕌₂ : …,
//    the universe 𝕌ℓ holding exactly the types of level ℓ — see THE
//    TOWER and the universe bridge
//  * A universe of mere propositions: Ω — impredicative, irrelevant,
//    every proposition a type of level 0 (see the Ω block)
//  * Quotient inductive-inductive types (QIITs): a SCHEME, one for
//    each well-formed signature 𝒮 — see "Quotient inductive-inductive
//    types". ℕ, (⊎) and (/) are its special cases, retained for now
//  * Coinductive types: a second SCHEME, dual to QIITs — see
//    "Coinductive types". Every ν 𝔽 is DERIVABLE (SUBSUMPTION in the
//    coinductive notes); the scheme is kept for structural identity
//    and one-step β

# Syntax

// signature identifier
x ::= <signature-entry-identifier>

// signature context (see DEFINITIONAL AND OPEN SIGNATURES)
Σ ::= ε
    | Σ (Γ ⊦ x ≔ t : t)     // definition
    | Σ (Γ ⊦ x : t)         // declaration (a HOLE — no definiens)
    | Σ (Γ ⊦ x ≔ 𝒮 qctx ℓ)  // QIIT signature definition (sig-qctx): what a
                            //   QIIT former carries, as x[σ]

// typing context
Γ, Θ, Ξ ::= ε      // empty typing context
    | Γ ▷ t        // extended typing context

// typing context substitution
σ, τ, ρ ::= ·           // empty substitution
     | σ, t          // extension substitution
     | σ ∘ σ         // composition substitution
     | id            // identity substitution
     | ↑             // weakening substitution

// label — its own syntactic class, disjoint from the signature
// identifiers x. A label is never bound and never substituted for.
l ::= <label>

// label list
l̄ ::= · | l l̄

// term — ONE sort: types and elements. A term is a TYPE when
// Γ ⊦ A type ℓ (for finite ℓ: when it is an element of 𝕌ℓ, the
// universe bridge). DISPLAY CONVENTION: the metavariables T, A,
// B, C range over terms standing in type position, t, a, b, e over
// terms in element position — one grammar, two reading aids.
t, a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, m, p, q, r, s, u, v, w, x, z, T, A, B, C, D, E, P, Q, R ::= x[σ]   // signature reference (σ NORMAL: sub-norm)
    | ☐ᵢ             // i-th element in the typing context
    | 𝕌ℓ             // the ℓ-th predicative universe
    | Ω              // universe of mere propositions
    | 𝟘              // empty type
    | 𝟙              // unit type
    | ℕ              // naturals
    | t → t          // dependent product (Π)
    | t × t          // dependent sum (Σ)
    | t ⊎ t          // non-dependent sum (disjoint union)
    | t / t          // quotient by an Ω-valued relation
    | λ t            // dependent product type introduction
    | t t            // dependent product type elimination
    | let t t        // let-expression (definiens, body)
    | t , t          // dependent sum type introduction
    | t .π₁          // dependent sum type elimination (1)
    | t .π₂          // dependent sum type elimination (2)
    | Rec l̄ Δ        // record type: the telescope Δ, a label per entry
    | ⟨l̄ ↪ ē⟩        // record: a label list and a spine, in parallel
    | t.l            // record projection at a label
    | inj₁ t         // non-dependent sum type introduction (left)
    | inj₂ t         // non-dependent sum type introduction (right)
    | ⊎-elim t t t   // non-dependent sum type elimination
                     //   (left case, right case, eliminee)
    | t ≡ t ∈ t      // equality PROPOSITION (the Ω block)
    | 𝟘-elim t
    | ()             // the only element of 𝟙
    | Z
    | S t
    | ℕ-elim t t t
    | class t        // quotient type introduction
    | quot-elim t t  // quotient type elimination
    | ∥t∥            // squash: proposition from a type (the Ω block)
    | ⋆              // the canonical proof of a true proposition
    | 𝒮.i ē          // the former at ENTRY i of a QIIT signature 𝒮, at
                     //   the spine ē: the sort 𝕤 at its index spine, or
                     //   the POINT constructor 𝕔, SATURATED (equation
                     //   constructors mint no term — el-qiit-path). 𝒮
                     //   is carried in the term, written out or a
                     //   reference x[σ], so QIIT equality is structural
    | ν 𝔽            // coinductive type at polynomial 𝔽 (carried)
    | out t          // coinductive observation (the ELIMINATOR)
    | corec 𝔽 t t    // corecursor (the INTRODUCTION: polynomial, coalgebra
                     //   body, seed — 𝔽 is CARRIED, like d̄ at 𝒮-elim:
                     //   el-nu-beta consumes map_𝔽, so the redex must be
                     //   self-contained. The carrier is NOT: a term carries
                     //   only what computation reads)

// universe — Ω, or a level of the tower: what a type of finite level
// is an element of (the universe bridge). Both are terms above; 𝒰 is only the METAVARIABLE for "either", used
// where a notion is stated at both alike — the targets of the
// displayed reading (the QIIT section), and the sorts of the checker
// (docs/NovaKernel.nspec).
𝒰 ::= Ω | 𝕌ℓ | _

// universe level — a natural number; ω is NOT one (it is an index of
// the levelled judgements only: LEVEL INDICES, conventions)
ℓ ::= <natural number>

// index: of a variable, of a weakening
i, j, k, n, 𝕤, 𝕔 ::= <natural number>   // 𝕤, 𝕔: a sort's, a constructor's POSITION in a signature

// (type) telescope
Δ ::= ε | t ◁ Δ

// (element) spine
ē, θ, ι, δ, ā, d̄, m̄, h̄ ::= · | t, ē
    | 𝒮-elim ē          // QIIT eliminator: THE SECTION of the displayed
                        //   algebra ē over ι(𝒮) (𝒮 CARRIED) — a spine of
                        //   the section telescope, see Elimination

// qiit-context
𝒮, Φ ::= ε | 𝒮 ▷ 𝔄 | x[σ]

// qiit-type
𝔄, 𝔅, 𝔎 ::= U               // the universe of codes
    | El 𝕥                  // decoding of a code 𝕥 : U
    | t ⇛ 𝔄                 // EXTERNAL Π — domain a Nova
                            //   type; binds a NOVA variable
    | El 𝕥 ⇛ 𝔄              // INDUCTIVE Π — binds a ToS
                                //   variable

// qiit-term
𝕥, 𝕦, 𝕧, 𝕝, 𝕣, 𝕖 ::= ⬡ᵢ     // ToS VARIABLE, de Bruijn
    | ⬡(i)                  // Same as above, just another notation
    | 𝕥 t                   // application to a Nova term
    | 𝕥 𝕥                   // application to a ToS term
    | λ 𝕥                   // external abstraction (binds
                            //   a NOVA variable)
    | 𝕥 ≡ 𝕥                 // equation CODE (in U, at the
                            //   sides' common El)

// qiit-sub
ς, τ ::= 𝕚𝕕 | ⇑             // the ToS's OWN substitution
         | ς ∘ ς            //   calculus, mirroring Nova's
         | ς, 𝕥             //   (the lift ς ▷ 𝔄 is derived: qsub-lift)

## Notation

// How the defined (meta-level) notions are written: alternatives added to
// the categories of the syntax. Each notion is defined where it is
// introduced.
Γ |= Γ · Δ | (Γ)
Δ |= Δ[σ] | Δ ▷ t | (Δ) | ⟦𝒮⟧ | ⟦𝒮⟧ | ⟦𝔄⟧ᵗ | ⟦Ψ⟧ | 𝔗ᴺ | ⟦𝒮⟧ᴰ 𝒰̄ | ⟦𝒮 ⊑ 𝒮⟧ᴰ 𝒰̄ | ⟦𝔗⟧ᴰ 𝒰̄ | ⟦𝒮⟧ˢ | ⟦𝒮 ⊑ 𝒮⟧ˢ
σ |= σ ▷ t | ↑ⁱ | ↑Δ | (σ) | σ, ē | ξ | ξ⁻¹ | ξ_Δ | ξᴰ | ξᴰ⁻¹ | υ | υᴰ | ξˢ | ξˢ⁻¹ | σ · Δ
ē |= ē[σ] | (ē) | t.l̄ | ☐(Δ) | ι(𝒮) | ι_𝒮(𝒮) | ē, t | ēᴺ | ēᵀ
l̄ |= (l̄)
ℓ |= ℓ + ℓ | ℓ ⊔ ℓ | (ℓ)
i |= (i) | i + i
t, w |= t[σ] | (t) | Γ‖ᵢ | 𝒰
    | ⟦𝔄⟧ | ⟦𝕥⟧ | ⟦𝔄⟧ᵉ | ⟦𝔄⟧ᴰ 𝒰 t | ⟦𝕥⟧ᴰ 𝒰̄ | ⟦𝔄⟧ˢ t t | ⟦𝕥⟧ˢ
    | t ē
    | ⌊𝔽⌋(t) | map_𝔽 t | map_{𝔽} t | lift_𝔽(t) | lift_{𝔽}(t) | Δ → t | λ Δ. t
𝔽 |= 𝔽[σ] | (𝔽)
𝒮 |= 𝒮[σ] | (𝒮) | 𝒮 · Ψ
Ψ |= Ψ[σ] | Ψ[ς] | (Ψ) | 𝔗ᵀ | 𝒮 ∖ 𝒮
𝔗 |= 𝔗[σ] | 𝔗[ς] | (𝔗) | 𝔄ᵗ
ī ::= · | t, ī | 𝕥, ī       // index spine: external and inductive arguments

// qiit-telescope: ToS entries over a qiit-context, as a telescope over a context
Ψ ::= ε | 𝔄 ◁ Ψ

// qiit-dual: the binders of a qiit-type, external or inductive
𝔗 ::= ε | t ◁ 𝔗 | El 𝕥 ◁ 𝔗
𝒰̄ ::= · | 𝒰, 𝒰̄ | 𝒰̄, 𝒰 | (𝒰̄)
𝒰 |= 𝒰̄‖ᵢ
𝔄 |= 𝔄[σ] | 𝔄[ς] | (𝔄) | 𝒮‖ᵢ | 𝔄ᵉ | 𝔗 ⇛ 𝔄
𝕥 |= 𝕥[σ] | 𝕥[ς] | (𝕥) | 𝕥 ī
ς |= (ς) | ς[σ] | ς ▷ 𝔄
ī |= (ī)

## Judgement forms

// The grammar of the judgements listed in Judgements below.
J ::= Σ sig | Γ ctx | Γ ≐ Γ ctx | Σ Γ ctx
    | σ : Γ ⇒ Γ | σ ≐ σ : Γ ⇒ Γ
    | σ : Γ ⇒ Γ norm | σ ≐ σ : Γ ⇒ Γ norm
    | Γ ⊦ t type ℓ | Γ ⊦ t type | Γ ⊦ t ≐ t type | Σ Γ ⊦ t type ℓ | Σ Γ ⊦ t type | Σ Γ ⊦ t ≐ t type
    | Γ ⊦ t : t | Γ ⊦ t ≐ t : t | Σ Γ ⊦ t : t | Σ Γ ⊦ t ≐ t : t
    | Γ ⊦ Δ tel ℓ | Γ ⊦ Δ tel | Γ ⊦ Δ ≐ Δ tel
    | Γ ⊦ ē : Δ | Γ ⊦ ē ≐ ē : Δ
    | (Γ ⊦ x ≔ t : t) ∈ Σ | (Γ ⊦ x : t) ∈ Σ | (Γ ⊦ x ≔ 𝒮 qctx ℓ) ∈ Σ | x ∉ Σ
    | l ∈ l̄ | l ∉ l̄ | l̄ distinct | |l̄| = |Δ| | Γ ⊦ t / l̄ ↪ Δ
    | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 qty ℓ | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 qty | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔄 ≐ 𝔄 qty
    | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ Ψ qtel ℓ | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ Ψ qtel | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ Ψ ≐ Ψ qtel | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔗 qdual | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝔗 ≐ 𝔗 qdual | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ ī : 𝔗 | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝕥 : 𝔄 | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝕥 ≐ 𝕥 : 𝔄 | Γ ⊦ ς : 𝒮 ⇒ 𝒮 | Γ ⊦ ς ≐ ς : 𝒮 ⇒ 𝒮
    | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx ℓ | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 ≐ 𝒮 qctx | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 ⊑ᵢ 𝒮 | Γ ⊦ 𝒮 ⊑ₛ 𝒮 | Σ Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx ℓ | Σ Γ ⊦ 𝒮 qctx | Σ Γ ⊦ 𝒮 ≐ 𝒮 qctx
    | Γ ⊦ 𝒰̄ targets 𝒮 | Γ ; 𝒮 ⊦ 𝒰 targets 𝔄 | 𝒰̄‖ᵢ = 𝒰
    | Γ ⊦ 𝔽 poly ℓ | Γ ⊦ 𝔽 poly | Γ ⊦ 𝔽 ≐ 𝔽 poly | ℓ ≼ ℓ

# Conventions: how to read the rules

// The rules below are presented COMPACTLY. Each convention comes with its
// mechanical expansion, so the fully explicit form of every rule is
// recoverable without judgement calls.
//
// * AMBIENT SIGNATURE. Every judgement is relative to a signature Σ.
//   The Σ prefix and the premise `Σ sig` are ELIDED everywhere; rules
//   that inspect or extend Σ (the sig rules, the x[σ] rules) name it
//   explicitly. Expansion: prefix every judgement with `Σ ⊦`/`Σ Γ ⊦`
//   as appropriate and add `Σ sig` as the first premise of every rule.
//   This is the one elision of the presentation; nothing else is left
//   unstated.
//
// * PRESUPPOSITIONS. A judgement form PRESUPPOSES the well-formedness
//   of its parts (the displays in Judgements): Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A
//   presupposes Γ ⊦ a₀ : A and Γ ⊦ a₁ : A, which presuppose
//   Γ ⊦ A type, which presupposes Γ ctx. A derivation of a form takes
//   its presuppositions for granted, and a rule is WELL-FORMED only if
//   its premises, with their own presuppositions, yield the
//   presuppositions of its conclusion — an obligation met by
//   construction, rule by rule, some of them through THE INVERSION
//   LEMMA, the one meta-theorem proved by induction (after the
//   judgement forms). A premise is therefore omitted when another premise
//   presupposes it or inversion recovers it — `Γ ▷ A ⊦ f : B` carries
//   `Γ ctx`, `Γ ⊦ A type` and `Γ ▷ A ⊦ B type` — and a rule in which a
//   part of the conclusion is mentioned by no premise's judgement
//   states that part's well-formedness explicitly (the substitution
//   rules that extend a context, `sub-ext` and its kin, carry
//   `Γ₁ ⊦ A type`). A premise is also KEPT where it is the handle of a
//   congruence (CONGRUENCE below): a part of the conclusion's subject
//   is well-formed by a premise of its own, so that the congruence has
//   an equation to state for it (`Γ ctx` in ctx-ext, `Γ ⊦ A type` in
//   code-eq). Eliminator motives, the middle subject of a transitivity
//   and the data of the coercion rules are genuine inputs and always
//   appear. Expansion: none is needed — what a rule shows is what it
//   says.
//
// * LEVEL INDICES. The index ℓ of a levelled judgement — type ℓ,
//   tel ℓ, qctx ℓ, qty ℓ, qtel ℓ, poly ℓ — ranges over ℕ ∪ {ω}, the
//   order ℓ ≼ ℓ′ being that of ℕ with ω above every level, and
//   ω + 1 = ω; the ω instance is WRITTEN WITHOUT THE INDEX (type, tel,
//   qctx, …): "A is a type", with no bound. A level ℓ occurring in a
//   TERM (𝕌ℓ) is a natural number, so a rule that mentions 𝕌ℓ has no ω
//   instance; ω is an index of the judgements and nothing else — not a
//   level, not a term, not a universe. Expansion: a rule with the index
//   ℓ is one rule per element of ℕ ∪ {ω} (per natural number, if ℓ
//   occurs in a term of the rule).
//
// * GROUPED CONCLUSIONS. A rule with several conclusions below the line
//   abbreviates one rule per conclusion (same premises).
//
// * UNARY AND BINARY. Every sort has a unary judgement, its
//   well-formedness, and a binary one, its EQUALITY — Γ ⊦ a : A and
//   Γ ⊦ a₀ ≐ a₁ : A, Γ ⊦ A type ℓ and Γ ⊦ A₀ ≐ A₁ type, Γ ctx and
//   Γ₀ ≐ Γ₁ ctx, and so on (Judgement forms). The binary judgement is
//   an EQUIVALENCE RELATION presupposing the unary of both sides; its
//   reflexivity is a RULE (el-refl, ty-refl), not a notation. The
//   equality PROPOSITION (a₀ ≡ a₁ ∈ A) is a separate thing, an element
//   of Ω that INTERNALIZES the binary judgement: el-eq-i takes the
//   judgement to its canonical inhabitant ⋆, el-reflect takes any
//   inhabitant back to the judgement (the Ω block). The POINTWISE
//   equalities (ctx, sub, sub-norm, tel, sp, qctx, qty, qtel, qdual,
//   qtm, qsub, poly) are given by the -cong rules of their formers
//   (CONGRUENCE below) — ctx-ext-cong, sub-ext-cong, tel-ext-cong,
//   sp-ext-cong, qctx-ext-cong, poly-prod-cong, … — together with the
//   extensionality rules of the two substitution calculi
//   (sub-extensional, qsub-extensional).
//
//   THE EQUIVALENCE structure of the element judgement is three RULES,
//   el-refl, el-sym and el-trans (Elements); the type judgement's are
//   ty-refl, ty-sym and ty-trans (Types). Transitivity's middle
//   subject, with its typing, is an input.
//   For the pointwise sorts, refl/sym/trans are ADMISSIBLE, pointwise
//   from these. Pointwise equality of constants and atoms (ε ≐ ε ctx,
//   id ≐ id, ↑ ≐ ↑, · ≐ ·, x[σ] ≐ x[σ], ...) are refl instances and
//   are not stated.
//
// * THE CONGRUENCE CONVENTION. A FORMER rule — a formation,
//   introduction or elimination rule, whose conclusion is a unary
//   judgement Γ ⊦ F(x̄) : T, Γ ⊦ F(x̄) type ℓ, Γ ⊦ F(x̄) tel ℓ, … — is
//   DISPLAYED in its unary form and STATES a second rule, its
//   CONGRUENCE, named <name>-cong: every metavariable of the term sort
//   (and of the substitution, telescope, spine, signature, ToS and
//   polynomial sorts) that occurs in the conclusion's subject is split
//   into a LEFT instance x₀ and a RIGHT instance x₁; each premise that
//   is a unary judgement of such a part — Γ′ ⊦ x : T′, Γ′ ⊦ x type ℓ,
//   Γ′ ⊦ x tel ℓ, Γ′ ⊦ x qctx ℓ, … — becomes the corresponding binary
//   judgement — Γ′ ⊦ x₀ ≐ x₁ : T′, Γ′ ⊦ x₀ ≐ x₁ type, Γ′ ⊦ x₀ ≐ x₁ tel,
//   Γ′ ⊦ x₀ ≐ x₁ qctx, … (the levelled forms become the LEVEL-FREE
//   equations); the conclusion becomes the binary judgement
//   Γ ⊦ F(x̄₀) ≐ F(x̄₁) : T, Γ ⊦ F(x̄₀) ≐ F(x̄₁) type, …; and every OTHER
//   occurrence — in the type slots of premises and conclusion, in the
//   contexts of the premises (the Γ ▷ A of a binder), in the
//   substitutions that instantiate a type — takes the RIGHT instance
//   (B[id, e₁], Γ ▷ A₁ ⊦ B₀ ≐ B₁; the left instance's premise is then
//   well-typed by the domain equation and coercion). Premises that are
//   not unary judgements of a subject part — side conditions,
//   Σ-membership, level orders, the label list l̄ (inert syntax,
//   compared for identity, never doubled), the ToS-zone premises that
//   classify an entry — are unchanged. A carried QIIT signature 𝒮 or
//   polynomial 𝔽 DOES double, through its own equation
//   (Γ ⊦ 𝒮₀ ≐ 𝒮₁ qctx — by name or by content, IDENTITY in the QIIT
//   section; Γ ⊦ 𝔽₀ ≐ 𝔽₁ poly), every other use of it in the premises
//   — an entry 𝒮‖ᵢ, an arity, the initial algebra — taking the RIGHT
//   one. Expansion: mechanical, by this clause; e.g. ty-pi states
//   ty-pi-cong,
//     Γ ⊦ A₀ ≐ A₁ type, Γ ▷ A₁ ⊦ B₀ ≐ B₁ type ⟹ Γ ⊦ A₀ → B₀ ≐ A₁ → B₁ type,
//   and el-sub states el-sub-cong, the MASTER congruence. Rules whose
//   conclusion is ALREADY an equation — the computation rules (β), the
//   uniqueness rules (η), the irrelevance, extensionality, lift,
//   restrict, coercion, injectivity and reflection rules — state no
//   second rule. One equation about a former may be STRONGER than its
//   congruence and is then a rule of its own (el-zero-e-cong).
//
// * ADMISSIBLE RULES. A rule whose bar comment begins ADM is
//   ADMISSIBLE: derivable from the rules without it (the comment says
//   how), and kept as a rule because the kernel checks it directly or
//   the docs cite it. Nothing below depends on an ADM rule beyond what
//   its justification gives.
//
// * COMPUTATION. A computation rule — a β-rule, a clause of a
//   substitution action or of a reading — concludes an equation ≐ like
//   any other rule; that the kernel reads it left to right is the
//   kernel's business. The syntax is INERT: no definition on raw
//   syntax stands behind any equation.

// * NAMES. Every rule carries a canonical name on its inference line,
//   following one homogeneous scheme — <class>-<former>-<kind> — where
//   the class prefix names the judgement class (ctx, sub, sub-norm,
//   ty, el, tel, sp, qctx, qty, qtel, qdual, qtm, qsub, poly), with
//   two exceptions within the element class: univ- for the universe
//   bridge, and code- for the rules proper to the universe of codes
//   (the Ω-formers and propositional extensionality). The former names
//   the connective or constructor, and the kind distinguishes
//   introduction (i), elimination (e), computation (beta), uniqueness
//   (eta), congruence (cong), injectivity (inj), substitution action
//   (sub), coercion (coe) and the level's monotonicity (lift). A -cong
//   name denotes the congruence a former rule STATES (THE CONGRUENCE
//   CONVENTION) and is not displayed; a -cong rule that IS displayed
//   is one stronger than the convention's. These names are the ones
//   docs/NovaKernel.nspec and the sources cite; there are no other
//   synonyms.

